---
title: 'Cross Lines'
description: 'Axes Cross Lines are lines or shaded areas in a $framework chart that provide additional info or thresholds to aid data analysis. Supports Cartesian and Polar Axes.'
---

Cross Lines are lines or shaded areas in a chart that denote additional information or thresholds, making them useful for data analysis.

## Adding Cross Lines

To add a Cross Line at a specific data value on an axis, use the `crossLines` property in the `axis` options.

A Cross Line can either be a `line` or a `range`, and multiple Cross Lines can be added to a single axis.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Adding Cross Lines" name="axis-cross-lines-adding" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        y: {
            position: 'left',
            type: 'number',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'line',
                    value: 11,
                },
            ],
        },
        x: {
            position: 'bottom',
            type: 'category',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'range',
                    range: ['Jun', 'Sep'],
                },
            ],
        },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- A `line` is displayed on the vertical axis at value 11.
- A `range` is displayed on the horizontal axis between June and September.
- The `value` or `range` values should be the same types as those displayed in the axis.

## Customising Cross Lines

{% chartExampleRunner title="Customising Cross Lines" name="axis-cross-lines-customising" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    crossLines: [
        {
            type: 'range',
            range: [new Date(2019, 4, 1), new Date(2019, 6, 1)],
            strokeWidth: 0,
            fill: '#7290C4',
            fillOpacity: 0.4,
            label: {
                text: 'Price Peak',
                placement: 'top',
                fontSize: 14,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this configuration:

- Properties such as `stroke`, `strokeWidth`, and `fill` are used to customise the Cross Line.
- A label is added, customised and placed in relation to the Cross Line. See [Label Placement](#label-placement) for more details.

## Label Placement

Use `label.placement` to choose where a label sits relative to its Cross Line. The available placements depend on the Cross Line type and on the axis it belongs to, and the default is `top`.

```js format="snippet"
{
    crossLines: [
        {
            type: 'line',
            value: 142.45,
            label: {
                text: 'Target',
                placement: ['inside-end', 'end'],
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this configuration:

- `inside-end` places the label on the line itself, at its end.
- `end` is used instead if the label doesn't fit there, placing it beyond the end of the line.

The first part of a placement names the edge or end the label sits against, and the second part positions the label along it. For example, `top-left` sits above the Cross Line aligned to its left, while `left-top` sits beside its left edge aligned to its top.

### Line Cross Lines

A `line` on a y axis runs horizontally and accepts the following placements:

- `top`, `bottom`, `top-left`, `top-right`, `bottom-left` and `bottom-right` sit above or below the line, inside the series area.
- `left` and `right` sit beyond the ends of the line, outside the series area.
- `inside` sits on the line at its midpoint, and `inside-left` and `inside-right` sit on the line at that end.

A `line` on an x axis runs vertically and accepts the following placements:

- `left`, `right`, `left-top`, `left-bottom`, `right-top` and `right-bottom` sit beside the line, inside the series area.
- `top` and `bottom` sit beyond the ends of the line, outside the series area.
- `inside` sits on the line at its midpoint, and `inside-top` and `inside-bottom` sit on the line at that end.

### Range Cross Lines

A `range` accepts every placement on either axis:

- `top`, `bottom`, `left` and `right` sit outside the band, centred on that edge.
- `top-left`, `top-right`, `bottom-left` and `bottom-right` sit outside the band's top or bottom edge, aligned to the named side.
- `left-top`, `left-bottom`, `right-top` and `right-bottom` sit outside the band's left or right edge, aligned to the named side.
- `inside` sits in the centre of the band, and `inside-top`, `inside-bottom`, `inside-left`, `inside-right`, `inside-top-left`, `inside-top-right`, `inside-bottom-left` and `inside-bottom-right` sit inside the band against that edge or corner.

### Text Direction

Every placement that names `left` or `right` has a twin that names `start` or `end` instead, such as `top-start` or `inside-end`:

- `start` and `end` resolve to `left` and `right` in a left-to-right chart, and are mirrored when [RTL Layout](./rtl/) is enabled.
- Placements that name `left` or `right` are not mirrored.
- On an x-axis `line`, `inside-start` and `inside-end` are the same as `inside-top` and `inside-bottom`, and are not mirrored.

### Fallback Placements

Providing `placement` as an ordered array lets the label fall back to the next placement when an earlier one collides with another element or doesn't fit in the chart:

- The first placement that fits is used.
- If none fits, the label uses the placement where it is least obstructed, preferring earlier placements when they are equally obstructed.
- A placement that doesn't apply to the Cross Line type and axis is ignored, and a warning lists the placements that do.

### Fitting Long Labels

Set `maxWidth` or `maxHeight` to limit the space the label text can take up. A label that does not fit is wrapped, truncated or shrunk.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Fitting Long Labels" name="label-fitting" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    crossLines: [
        {
            type: 'line',
            value: 250,
            label: {
                text: 'Annual Revenue Target: £250k per Month',
                placement: 'top-left',
                maxWidth: 120,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- Click the buttons to switch between wrapping, truncating and shrinking the labels.
- `maxWidth` limits the label text to 120px wide, so by default the text wraps onto multiple lines.
- `wrapping` sets how text wraps. Set it to `'never'` to keep the label on one line.
- `truncate` controls whether text that still does not fit is truncated with an ellipsis.
- `minimumFontSize` lets the label shrink from its `fontSize` to fit, before it is truncated.
- The limits apply to the label text before any `rotation`, so a rotated label is fitted along its own direction.

## Polar Axes Cross Lines

Cross Lines are also available for Polar Axes. They share the line and range options of Cartesian Cross Lines, but the label options differ by axis: radius axis labels are positioned with `positionAngle`, and angle axis labels support the common label options only.

{% note %}
Polar Cross Lines are not included in `CrossLinesModule`. Register `PolarCrossLinesModule` separately when using [module imports](./module-registry/).
{% /note %}

{% chartExampleRunner title="Polar Axis Angle Crosslines" name="polar-axes-crosslines-angle" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        angle: {
            type: 'angle-category',
            shape: 'circle',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'range',
                    range: ['Technical Skills', 'Communication'],
                    label: {
                        text: 'Valuable Skills',
                    },
                },
            ],
        },
        radius: {
            type: 'radius-number',
            shape: 'circle',
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'line',
                    stroke: 'red',
                    value: 6,
                    label: {
                        text: 'Minimal\nRequirement',
                        positionAngle: 180,
                    },
                },
            ],
        },
    },
}
```

## Theming Cross Lines

[Theme overrides](./themes/#overrides) style every Cross Line on an axis type. Add a `line` or `range` object to style only the Cross Lines of that type.

```js format="snippet"
{
    theme: {
        overrides: {
            common: {
                axes: {
                    number: {
                        crossLines: {
                            strokeWidth: 2,
                            line: { stroke: '#d62728', lineDash: [4, 4] },
                            range: { fill: '#7290C4', fillOpacity: 0.2 },
                        },
                    },
                },
            },
        },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

- `strokeWidth` applies to every Cross Line.
- The `line` and `range` options take precedence over the options for every Cross Line, and options set on a Cross Line take precedence over both.

